    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d486">

                <pb facs="0978" n="944" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v08/pg944"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Eisenhower
                    Library, Eisenhower papers, Whitman
                    file</note>
                <head>No. 486<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the President to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-06-04">June 4, 1954</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I was visited day before yesterday<anchor n="1" xml:id="d486-f1"/> by Prime
                    Minister Menderes of Turkey, who brought up a number of
                    subjects, the most of which I assume will be discussed in the conferences he is
                    having with your Department and with FOA.<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d486-f2"/></p>
                <p>One general subject that came up for discussion impressed me. It was the attitude
                    of the Prime Minister—and his Government—toward the need for strengthening the
                    “Southern Flank Alliance.” He implied that the Turkish-Pakistan Alliance should
                    be strengthened by including several of the intervening nations, including
                    Afghanistan. He also believes that in this organization we should include Italy.
                    I do not think that he meant that Italy should be detached from the Gruenther organization, but that it should be
                    included in the Southern organization in such fashion that it would form sort of
                    a pivot between the two fronts, much as Turkey itself now forms a link between
                        NATO and the countries to the eastward.</p>
                <p>Pursuing this line of thought further, he pointed out that it would, of course,
                    be a very fine thing for the safety of the region if Yugoslavia could be
                    included in NATO, but quickly acknowledged that
                    under present conditions such a development is impossible. He volunteered that
                    he was going to do all he could to promote a peaceful solution of the Trieste
                    question so that with the Yugoslavs standing shoulder to shoulder with Turkey
                    and Greece, Italy would not only be rendered much safer from land attack, but
                    could devote its energies and reserves where needed.</p>
                <closer><signed>D.D.E.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
